SMEMProbe - can the VideoCore write pictures straight into our memory?
=====================================================================

Today the hardware decoder (vcdec, h264_vchiq) and encoder use MMAL's plain
mode: every decoded 1080p picture (3 MB) is sent from the VideoCore to
RISC OS by bulk transfer, and the VideoCore keeps its own copy of every
buffer in its memory (gpu_mem). That copy is why a second 1080p decoder,
or a 1080p decoder and an encoder, run out of room at gpu_mem=128.

Linux avoids both: its "vc-sm-cma" driver hands the VideoCore ordinary
memory by a handle (through the firmware's "SMEM" service), and the decoder
writes its pictures straight into it ("zero copy"). Nothing is transferred,
and little gpu_mem is used. RISC OS has no client for that service. This
probe finds out, step by step, whether one works:

  1. the "SMEM" service opened over VCHIQ;
  2. 64 KB of our memory imported into it: a VideoCore handle back (then
     freed);
  3. the 360p clip decoded with zero copy: every picture written straight
     into our memory, then checked against FFmpeg's;
  4. the 1080p clip timed with zero copy, then the usual way (vcdec), to
     compare.

Running it (please restart first, then send back Result):
  Probe    The four steps. Writes Result.
  Verbose  The first three, every message logged. Writes ResultV. Run it
           only if Probe stops part way (restart before it).

If a step is refused, the report says so and stops there: that is an
answer too. If the machine stops responding, switch it off and on again,
and send whatever Result holds.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi 4 with the VCHIQ module (in the ROM) and
gpu_mem=128 or more; RISC OS 5.23 or later (Physical Memory Pools).
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2. The clips are made from
FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
